{"surah": 92, "verses": [{"ayah": 1, "text": "وَٱلَّيْلِ إِذَا يَغْشَىٰ ", "translation": "By the night when it covers[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلَّيْلِ", "translit": "ِlْyَّlٱَw", "root": "ليل", "latinTranslit": "wa-al-layli"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "idhā"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَغْشَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَhsْغَy", "root": "غشو", "latinTranslit": "yaghshā"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "By the enshrouding night", "clearyTranslation": "By the enshrouding night"}, {"ayah": 2, "text": "وَٱلنَّهَارِ إِذَا تَجَلَّىٰ ", "translation": "And [by] the day when it appears", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلنَّهَارِ", "translit": "ِrāَhَّnlٱَw", "root": "نهر", "latinTranslit": "wal-nahāri"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "idhā"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "تَجَلَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّlَjgَt", "root": "جلو", "latinTranslit": "tajallā"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "by the radiant day", "clearyTranslation": "by the radiant day"}, {"ayah": 3, "text": "وَمَا خَلَقَ ٱلذَّكَرَ وَٱلْأُنثَىٰٓ ", "translation": "And [by] He who created the male and female,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wamā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "خَلَقَ", "translit": "َqَlَhk", "root": "خلق", "latinTranslit": "khalaqa"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلذَّكَرَ", "translit": "َrَkَّhdlٱ", "root": "ذكر", "latinTranslit": "l-dhakara"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَٱلْأُنثَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَhtnُaʾْlٱَw", "root": "انث", "latinTranslit": "wal-unthā"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "by His creation of male and female", "clearyTranslation": "by His creation of male and female"}, {"ayah": 4, "text": "إِنَّ سَعْيَكُمْ لَشَتَّىٰ ", "translation": "Indeed, your efforts are diverse.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "سَعْيَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَyْعَs", "root": "سعي", "latinTranslit": "saʿyakum"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَشَتَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّtَhsَl", "root": "شتت", "latinTranslit": "lashattā"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "The ways you take differ greatly", "clearyTranslation": "The ways you take differ greatly"}, {"ayah": 5, "text": "فَأَمَّا مَنْ أَعْطَىٰ وَٱتَّقَىٰ ", "translation": "As for he who gives and fears Allāh", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَأَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَaʾَf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fa-ammā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَنْ", "translit": "ْnَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "man"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَعْطَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَṭْعَaʾ", "root": "عطو", "latinTranslit": "aʿṭā"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَٱتَّقَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَqَّtٱَw", "root": "وقي", "latinTranslit": "wa-ittaqā"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "There is the one who gives, who is mindful of God", "clearyTranslation": "There is the one who gives, who is mindful of God"}, {"ayah": 6, "text": "وَصَدَّقَ بِٱلْحُسْنَىٰ ", "translation": "And believes in the best [reward].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَصَدَّقَ", "translit": "َqَّdَṣَw", "root": "صدق", "latinTranslit": "waṣaddaqa"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بِٱلْحُسْنَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَnْsُḥْlٱِb", "root": "حسن", "latinTranslit": "bil-ḥus'nā"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "who testifies to goodness––", "clearyTranslation": "who testifies to goodness––"}, {"ayah": 7, "text": "فَسَنُيَسِّرُهُۥ لِلْيُسْرَىٰ ", "translation": "We will ease him toward ease.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَسَنُيَسِّرُهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhُrِّsَyُnَsَf", "root": "يسر", "latinTranslit": "fasanuyassiruhu"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لِلْيُسْرَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَrْsُyْlِl", "root": "يسر", "latinTranslit": "lil'yus'rā"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "We shall smooth his way towards ease", "clearyTranslation": "We shall smooth his way towards ease"}, {"ayah": 8, "text": "وَأَمَّا مَنۢ بَخِلَ وَٱسْتَغْنَىٰ ", "translation": "But as for he who withholds and considers himself free of need", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَaʾَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-ammā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَنۢ", "translit": "ۢnَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "man"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بَخِلَ", "translit": "َlِhkَb", "root": "بخل", "latinTranslit": "bakhila"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَٱسْتَغْنَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَnْغَtْsٱَw", "root": "غني", "latinTranslit": "wa-is'taghnā"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "There is the one who is miserly, who is self-satisfied", "clearyTranslation": "There is the one who is miserly, who is self-satisfied"}, {"ayah": 9, "text": "وَكَذَّبَ بِٱلْحُسْنَىٰ ", "translation": "And denies the best [reward],", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَكَذَّبَ", "translit": "َbَّhdَkَw", "root": "كذب", "latinTranslit": "wakadhaba"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بِٱلْحُسْنَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَnْsُḥْlٱِb", "root": "حسن", "latinTranslit": "bil-ḥus'nā"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "who denies goodness––", "clearyTranslation": "who denies goodness––"}, {"ayah": 10, "text": "فَسَنُيَسِّرُهُۥ لِلْعُسْرَىٰ ", "translation": "We will ease him toward difficulty.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَسَنُيَسِّرُهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhُrِّsَyُnَsَf", "root": "يسر", "latinTranslit": "fasanuyassiruhu"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لِلْعُسْرَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَrْsُعْlِl", "root": "عسر", "latinTranslit": "lil'ʿus'rā"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "We shall smooth his way towards hardship", "clearyTranslation": "We shall smooth his way towards hardship"}, {"ayah": 11, "text": "وَمَا يُغْنِى عَنْهُ مَالُهُۥٓ إِذَا تَرَدَّىٰٓ ", "translation": "And what will his wealth avail him when he falls?[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wamā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يُغْنِى", "translit": "àِnْغُy", "root": "غني", "latinTranslit": "yugh'nī"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَنْهُ", "translit": "ُhْnَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿanhu"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَالُهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhُlāَm", "root": "مول", "latinTranslit": "māluhu"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "idhā"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "تَرَدَّىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَّdَrَt", "root": "ردي", "latinTranslit": "taraddā"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "and his wealth will not help him as he falls", "clearyTranslation": "and his wealth will not help him as he falls"}, {"ayah": 12, "text": "إِنَّ عَلَيْنَا لَلْهُدَىٰ ", "translation": "Indeed, [incumbent] upon Us is guidance.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عَلَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَlَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿalaynā"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَلْهُدَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَdُhْlَl", "root": "هدي", "latinTranslit": "lalhudā"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "Our part is to provide guidance––", "clearyTranslation": "Our part is to provide guidance––"}, {"ayah": 13, "text": "وَإِنَّ لَنَا لَلْءَاخِرَةَ وَٱلْأُولَىٰ ", "translation": "And indeed, to us belongs the Hereafter and the first [life].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-inna"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَنَا", "translit": "āَnَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lanā"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَلْءَاخِرَةَ", "translit": "َẗَrِhkāَءْlَl", "root": "اخر", "latinTranslit": "lalākhirata"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَٱلْأُولَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlwُaʾْlٱَw", "root": "اول", "latinTranslit": "wal-ūlā"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "this world and the next belong to Us––", "clearyTranslation": "this world and the next belong to Us––"}, {"ayah": 14, "text": "فَأَنذَرْتُكُمْ نَارًا تَلَظَّىٰ ", "translation": "So I have warned you of a Fire which is blazing.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَأَنذَرْتُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُtْrَhdnَaʾَf", "root": "نذر", "latinTranslit": "fa-andhartukum"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "نَارًا", "translit": "āًrāَn", "root": "نور", "latinTranslit": "nāran"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "تَلَظَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّẓَlَt", "root": "لظي", "latinTranslit": "talaẓẓā"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "so I warn you about the raging Fire", "clearyTranslation": "so I warn you about the raging Fire"}, {"ayah": 15, "text": "لَا يَصْلَىٰهَآ إِلَّا ٱلْأَشْقَى ", "translation": "None will [enter to] burn therein except the most wretched one", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَصْلَىٰهَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhٰàَlْṣَy", "root": "صلي", "latinTranslit": "yaṣlāhā"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "illā"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْأَشْقَى", "translit": "àَqْhsَaʾْlٱ", "root": "شقو", "latinTranslit": "l-ashqā"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "in which none but the most wicked one will burn", "clearyTranslation": "in which none but the most wicked one will burn"}, {"ayah": 16, "text": "ٱلَّذِى كَذَّبَ وَتَوَلَّىٰ ", "translation": "Who had denied and turned away.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhī"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَذَّبَ", "translit": "َbَّhdَk", "root": "كذب", "latinTranslit": "kadhaba"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَتَوَلَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّlَwَtَw", "root": "ولي", "latinTranslit": "watawallā"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "who denied [the truth], and turned away", "clearyTranslation": "who denied [the truth], and turned away"}, {"ayah": 17, "text": "وَسَيُجَنَّبُهَا ٱلْأَتْقَى ", "translation": "But the righteous one will avoid it –", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَسَيُجَنَّبُهَا", "translit": "āَhُbَّnَjgُyَsَw", "root": "جنب", "latinTranslit": "wasayujannabuhā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْأَتْقَى", "translit": "àَqْtَaʾْlٱ", "root": "وقي", "latinTranslit": "l-atqā"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "The most pious one will be spared this––", "clearyTranslation": "The most pious one will be spared this––"}, {"ayah": 18, "text": "ٱلَّذِى يُؤْتِى مَالَهُۥ يَتَزَكَّىٰ ", "translation": "[He] who gives [from] his wealth to purify himself", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhī"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يُؤْتِى", "translit": "àِtْwʾُy", "root": "اتي", "latinTranslit": "yu'tī"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَالَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَlāَm", "root": "مول", "latinTranslit": "mālahu"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَتَزَكَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّkَzَtَy", "root": "زكو", "latinTranslit": "yatazakkā"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "who gives his wealth away as self-purification", "clearyTranslation": "who gives his wealth away as self-purification"}, {"ayah": 19, "text": "وَمَا لِأَحَدٍ عِندَهُۥ مِن نِّعْمَةٍ تُجْزَىٰٓ ", "translation": "And not [giving] for anyone who has [done him] a favor to be rewarded[1] –", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wamā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لِأَحَدٍ", "translit": "ٍdَḥَaʾِl", "root": "احد", "latinTranslit": "li-aḥadin"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عِندَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَdnِع", "root": "عند", "latinTranslit": "ʿindahu"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "نِّعْمَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَmْعِّn", "root": "نعم", "latinTranslit": "niʿ'matin"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "تُجْزَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَzْjgُt", "root": "جزي", "latinTranslit": "tuj'zā"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "not to return a favour to anyone", "clearyTranslation": "not to return a favour to anyone"}, {"ayah": 20, "text": "إِلَّا ٱبْتِغَآءَ وَجْهِ رَبِّهِ ٱلْأَعْلَىٰ ", "translation": "But only seeking the face [i.e., acceptance] of his Lord, Most High.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "illā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱبْتِغَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَغِtْbٱ", "root": "بغي", "latinTranslit": "ib'tighāa"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَجْهِ", "translit": "ِhْjgَw", "root": "وجه", "latinTranslit": "wajhi"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "رَبِّهِ", "translit": "ِhِّbَr", "root": "ربب", "latinTranslit": "rabbihi"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْأَعْلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlْعَaʾْlٱ", "root": "علو", "latinTranslit": "l-aʿlā"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "but for the sake of his Lord the Most High––", "clearyTranslation": "but for the sake of his Lord the Most High––"}, {"ayah": 21, "text": "وَلَسَوْفَ يَرْضَىٰ ", "translation": "And he is going to be satisfied.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَسَوْفَ", "translit": "َfْwَsَlَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "walasawfa"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَرْضَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَḍْrَy", "root": "رضو", "latinTranslit": "yarḍā"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "and he will be well pleased", "clearyTranslation": "and he will be well pleased"}], "tafsir": {"5": [{"arabic": "بالترغيب بالخير وتيسير الأمر عليه وإليه الإشارة بقوله تعالى فأما من أعطى واتقى وصدق بالحسنى فسنيسره لليسرى وفي مثل هذا القلب يشرق نور المصباح من مكشاة الربوبية حتى لا يخفي فيه الشرك الخفي الذي هو أخفى من دبيب النملة السوداء في الليلة الظلماء فلا يخفى على هذا النور خافية ولا يروج عليه شيء من مكايد الشيطان بل يقف الشيطان ويوحي زخرف القول غرورا فلا يلتفت إليه وهذا القلب بعد طهارته من المهلكات يصير على القرب معمورا بالمنجيات التي سنذكرها من الشكر والصبر والخوف والرجاء والفقر والزهد والمحبة والرضا و", "english": "And he frequently used to say: \"O Turner of the hearts, make my heart firm upon Your religion.\" They said: \"Do you fear, O Messenger of Allah?\" He said: \"And what gives me security, while the heart is between two fingers of the fingers of the All-Merciful, and He turns it however He wills?\" (2) In another wording: \"If He wills, He makes it stand firm, and if He wills, He makes it deviate.\" And he (peace and blessings be upon him) struck three parables for it. He said: \"The parable of the heart is like a sparrow that flips about every hour.\" (3) And he (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"The parable of the heart in its overturning is like a cooking pot when it has gathered its boiling.\" (4) And he said: \"The parable of the heart is like a feather in an empty land that the winds turn over, back and front.\" (5) These overturnings and the marvels of Allah the Exalted's handiwork in its fluctuation — from where no [mere] knowledge can guide to it — are not known except by those who keep watch and observe their own states with Allah the Exalted.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 610", "section_title_en": "The Wonders of the Heart: Its States of Stability, Inclination, and Fluctuation Between the Two Attracting Forces", "section_index": 610, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn a thing over, to revolve it, to divert it from one face to another; the heart is so named for its constant turning and fluctuation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart's perpetual overturning is its very essence; its states are never fixed, and its rectitude or corruption follows its turning.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" — physical overturning. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; the heart's fluctuation between the two attractants is a real, continuous overturning.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The periodic structure of the opening sentence, with its cascading circumstantial clauses, requires careful tracking of the governing verb and its dependent states to preserve the causal sequence of the heart's affliction and response.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Insight"}], "14": [{"arabic": "للكافرين وقال تعالى فأنذرتكم نارا تلظى لا يصلاها إلا الأشقى الذي كذب وتولى وقال عز وجل وإن ربك لذوا مغفرة للناس على ظلمهم ويقال أن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم لم يزل يسأل في أمته حتى قيل له أما ترضى وقد أنزلت عليك هذه الآية وإن ربك لذو مغفرة للناس ظلمهم (2) وفي تفسير قوله تعالى {ولسوف يعطيك ربك فترضى} قال لا يرضى محمد وواحد من أمته في النار وكان أبو جعفر محمد بن علي يقول أنتم أهل العراق تقولون أرجى آية في كتاب الله عز وجل قوله {قل يا عبادي الذين أسرفوا على أنفسهم لا تقنطوا من رحمة الله} ا", "english": "As for the reports (al-akhbār), Abū Mūsā narrated from him, peace be upon him, that he said: \"My community is a community shown mercy; there is no punishment upon it in the Hereafter. God hastened its punishment in this world — the earthquakes and the trials. And when the Day of Resurrection comes, a man from the People of the Book will be given to every man of my community, and it will be said: 'This is your ransom from the Fire.'\" (3)", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 1033", "section_title_en": "The Book of Hope (Kitāb al-Rajā') — Section 1033: The Second Art — Surveying the Verses and Reports Concerning Hope", "section_index": 1033, "anchors": "- Root: وجود (w-j-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of \"finding\" and \"existence\"; here in the sense of what is present and available.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Existence as that which is found and realized; in theological context, the given order of creation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) sense of \"being found\"; metaphorical (majāz) extension to the entirety of created things.\n- Root: رجا (r-j-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To hope, to expect with desire; the opposite of despair (ya's).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Hope as a theological virtue — the heart's expectation of divine mercy, balanced against fear (khawf).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: expectation of a desired outcome; metaphorical: the soul's reliance on divine generosity.\n- Root: غفر (gh-f-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To cover, to shield, to pardon; the root implies veiling the fault.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Divine forgiveness as the covering of sins and the effacing of their consequence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: covering; metaphorical: God's remission of punishment.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — used to emphasize exclusive attributes of divine mercy and forgiveness.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "15": [{"arabic": "مكذبا ولقوله تعالى {لا يصلاها إلا الأشقى الذي كذب وتولى} وهذا حصر وإثبات ونفي ولقوله تعالى {من جاء بالحسنة فله خير منها وهم من فزع يومئذ آمنون} فالإيمان رأس الحسنات ولقوله تعالى {والله يحب المحسنين} وقال تعالى {إنا لا نضيع أجر من أحسن عملا} ولا حجة لهم في ذلك فإنه حيث ذكر الإيمان في هذه الآيات أريد به الإيمان مع العمل إذ بينا أن الإيمان قد يطلق ويراد به الإسلام وهو الموافقة بالقلب والقول والعمل ودليل هذا التأويل أخبار كثيرة في معاقبة العاصين ومقادير العقاب وق", "english": "Likewise, abstaining from the unlawful is among the things obligatory upon one for God, just like the prayer, for his saying (peace and blessings be upon him): \"Seeking the lawful is an obligation after [other] obligations.\" This does not contradict our saying that inheritance is a judgment of Islam, which is submission (istislām). Rather, complete submission is that which encompasses both the outward and the inward. These are jurisprudential (fiqhī) matters of probability (ẓannī), built upon the outward meanings of words, general statements (ʿumūmāt), and analogies (aqyisah). It is not fitting for one deficient in the sciences to think that certainty (qaṭʿ) is required here, merely because it is customary to present such topics in the discipline of theology (Kalām), where certainty is sought. For he who looks to customs and formalities in the sciences will never prosper.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 99", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 99: On the Distinction, Overlap, and Synonymy of Faith (Īmān) and Submission (Islām), and Their Worldly and Otherworldly Judgments", "section_index": 99, "anchors": "- Root: ايم (A-Y-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of remaining unmarried, or a state of being without a spouse; extended metaphorically to describe one who is solitary or without support.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأيامى: جمع أيم، وهي المرأة التي لا بعل لها... والحرب مأيمة، أي: يفرق بين الزوج والزوجة.\" The root carries the sense of separation or a state of lacking union, which informs the theological discussion of what constitutes the essential bond of faith.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes the state of being unmarried; metaphorical (Majaz) usage extends to anything that separates or leaves one bereft. This distinction between literal and metaphorical application is central to the chapter's method.\n- Root: قلب (Q-L-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn, overturn, or transform a thing from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه... والانقلاب: الانصراف.\" The heart (qalb) is so named for its constant fluctuation and turning. In theological terms, it is the seat of taṣdīq (assent), the immaterial faculty of cognition and conviction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is physical turning; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is \"قلب الأمور\" — to deliberate, scrutinize, and manage affairs. This supports the distinction between the heart's internal act of assent and the external acts of the limbs.\n- Root: عمل (ʿ-A-M-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Any deed or action performed with purpose and intent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العمل: كل فعل يكون من الحيوان بقصد، فهو أخص من الفعل.\" Work is more specific than mere action, as it implies deliberate, intentional agency. It is used for both righteous and sinful deeds, and is the external manifestation of internal conviction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage refers to physical labor or deeds. The chapter explores whether \"work\" is an integral part of the reality of faith or a separate, consequent requirement.\n- Root: صدق (S-D-Q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be truthful, to speak the truth, to verify.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصدق والكذب أصلهما في القول... ولا يكونان بالقصد الأول إلا في القول.\" Truth and falsehood are primarily properties of speech and reports. Taṣdīq (assent) is the heart's affirmation of the truth of a report, which is the core reality of Īmān.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is truthful speech; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is \"صادق الحملة\" — one who is true and firm in battle or resolve. This shows that taṣdīq is not merely verbal but a firm, internal resolution.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication). This rule governs how restriction (ḥaṣr) is achieved in Arabic, often through particles like \"innamā\" or through contextual specification (takhṣīṣ). The chapter's core argument hinges on how terms like Īmān and Islām are semantically restricted, expanded, or made synonymous through contextual usage (majāz), requiring precise parsing of the predicate and its scope.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Insight"}]}, "wordTiming": {"minshawi": {"1": [[1, 330, 1597], [2, 1597, 2489], [3, 2489, 4080]], "92:1": [[1, 330, 1597], [2, 1597, 2489], [3, 2489, 4080]], "2": [[1, 200, 2538], [2, 2538, 3646], [3, 3646, 5400]], "92:2": [[1, 200, 2538], [2, 2538, 3646], [3, 3646, 5400]], "3": [[1, 210, 1427], [2, 1427, 2095], [3, 2095, 3147], [4, 3147, 5990]], "92:3": [[1, 210, 1427], [2, 1427, 2095], [3, 2095, 3147], [4, 3147, 5990]], "4": [[1, 40, 1012], [2, 1012, 2942], [3, 2942, 5260]], "92:4": [[1, 40, 1012], [2, 1012, 2942], [3, 2942, 5260]], "5": [[1, 200, 1818], [2, 1818, 2274], [3, 2274,